# Agent workflow

Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONFIG.md`, and `docs/ai/AGENT_WORKFLOW.md` before doing
non-trivial work in this repo. They are the source of truth; this rule is a summary, not a
replacement.

- Plan before implementing. For any non-trivial task, write a plan and stop. If the task naturally
  decomposes into independent pieces, propose that breakdown as a numbered list rather than picking
  one — see `AGENTS.md` → "Splitting a task into sub-tasks".
- Do not create branches, write implementation code, or run install/update scripts until the human
  replies with the exact phrase `approve` — unless the task was given via
  `/github_kit <task>`, which is itself the approval for that task; see
  `.cursor/rules/github-kit-command.mdc` and `docs/ai/AGENT_WORKFLOW.md` → "Fast-path trigger:
  /github_kit". If a numbered sub-task breakdown was proposed, also accept `approve all` (run every
  sub-task in sequence without stopping again) or `approve 1,3` (only the listed numbers). Plain
  `approve` never covers targeting the production branch directly — that needs a separate,
  explicit `approve main` plus the matching marker line in the PR body; see `AGENTS.md` →
  "Production-branch gate (`approve main`)".
- Once approved: create the GitHub issue with `scripts/project/create_agent_issue.sh --agent <name>
  --area <area> --risk <Low|Medium|High> --environment <env>` (always pass those four flags
  explicitly — fills assignee/labels/milestone and adds it to the Project; add `--parent
  <parent-issue>` for a sub-task), then create the task's **git worktree** with
  `scripts/project/publish_agent_branch.sh` (forks a *real* worktree from `origin/<base>`, default
  prefix `agent/`) **before** writing implementation code — `cd` into the worktree path it prints
  and read the `WORKTREE.md` it drops there before doing anything. Every task runs in its own
  worktree; agents work this repo in parallel and must never share a working tree. Implement only
  what was approved, validate with the commands in `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONFIG.md`, and open a **draft**
  PR with `scripts/project/create_agent_pr.sh` (same four flags, plus `--agent-run`/`--handoff`)
  using `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`.
- After the PR merges, clean up after yourself with
  `scripts/project/cleanup_merged_branches.sh --branch <branch>` — it removes the worktree, deletes
  the local branch, and closes the linked issue, but only when the merge is real and nothing
  unsaved would be lost (a `SKIPPED` line means investigate, don't force it). See `AGENTS.md` →
  "Branch and worktree rules".
- Keep the Project's `Status`, `Validation`, and `Last Agent Update` fields current by calling
  `scripts/project/sync_project_fields.sh <checkpoint> <issue-url> [text]` at each workflow
  checkpoint as you move through phases, rather than calling `project_set_status.sh`/
  `project_set_text.sh` directly.
- Declare real issue/PR relationships in the body (`Blocked by #N`, `Blocks #N`, `Part of #N`) or
  write `Relationships: none declared`; rely on `--assignee`/`--reviewer` (set by the scripts
  above) for notifications rather than assuming a separate subscribe step exists. See `AGENTS.md`
  → "GitHub relationships and development links" / "Notifications and participation".
- Before stopping mid-task (context limit, end of session, or handing off to a different agent or
  tool), write `docs/ai/handoffs/issue-<number>.md` describing current state, what's left, and the
  next step.
- Never chase CI. It only runs for production-bound changes or an explicit human dispatch, so a
  preview PR with no checks at all is the expected outcome — don't fetch check runs, wait, poll,
  subscribe to PR activity, or report "CI didn't run"/"checks are missing"/"the PR is red"
  anywhere, and never dispatch a workflow yourself. Exceptions: the human explicitly asks, or the
  change targets the production branch. See `AGENTS.md` → "CI expectations — don't chase checks".
- A human approves plans and merges PRs — never act past those boundaries on your own judgment.
  `AGENTS.md` → "Stop-and-ask gates" is the complete, enumerated list of moments that need a human
  reply; anything not on that list is your judgment call, not a reason to stop and ask.
- This repo's reusable workflows auto-track `pzoli6/github-kit@main` by default — local bootstrap
  files (this rule included) only refresh when `/github_kit_update` or the update script runs; see
  `.cursor/rules/github-kit-command.mdc`.
